  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 215 PM EST Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather continues into the middle of this week \nwith a gradual warming trend. \n\nA well-defined area of high pressure is centered just offshore of \nthe NJ coastline this afternoon. An unsettled pattern is in place in \nthe TN Valley vicinity, with a shortwave traversing through these \nareas. A deeper/stronger trough is traversing the western CONUS. A \nlarge area of cloudiness is associated with both of these systems, \nand waves of mid- to high-level clouds continue to move over our \narea and will continue to do so for the next few days. Otherwise, \nit's another beautiful fall day locally, with air temps in the mid \n60s and dew points in the 40s. Expect another chilly night, but not \nexcessively so, with lows temps ranging through the 40s \n(potentially some upper 30s well inland and on the MD Eastern \nShore). These temps are generally a few degrees too warm for \nappreciable frost concerns. \n\nThat shortwave will move across the local area Monday, followed by a \nbuilding ridge over the eastern/northeastern CONUS Tuesday into \nWednesday. Other than variable cloudiness, dry weather is expected \nto continue due to limited moisture at the sfc. A warming trend also \nbegins Monday (highs mid 60s to around 70 F) and continues into \nTuesday (highs lower to mid 70s) and Wednesday (highs mid to upper \n70s). Depending on cloud coverage, could also approach 80 F across \nportions of interior srn VA and NE NC Wednesday. Overnight lows also \ngradually moderate from the 40s Monday night to around 60 F \nWednesday night. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A chance for showers returns Thursday and Friday, \nbut confidence remains low. Above average temperatures are also \nexpected to continue through at least the first few days of November.\n\nA strong low pressure system at the surface and aloft will initially \nmove from the Rockies into the northern Plains and Midwest early in \nthe week, while a trailing cold front eventually tries to advance \ntoward the area in the Thursday-Friday timeframe. Shower chances \nreturn by Thursday into Thursday night as the trailing cold front \ngets close to the area. Unsurprisingly, the downward trend in precip \ncoverage and amounts has continued with the 12z deterministic and \nensemble runs, with the ECMWF/EPS suite similar to the 00z and the \nGFS/GEFS suite drier. Probabilities for >0.5\" generally remain in \nthe 30-50% range, but are a little higher over the lower MD Eastern \nShore where negligibly-more favorable forcing will reside. Overall, \nthe local area still appears to reside in a relative QPF minima as \nthe appreciable height falls are displaced well N of the region. The \n40-50% PoPs remain in place during this timeframe, but this should \nnot be a washout and more like a 'scattered showers' regime. Highs \nremain at least in the low-mid 70s through the end of the week, with \ndew points potentially rising well into the 60s by late week. As of \nnow, the front is forecast to eventually move through sometime \nFriday. Uncertainty remains beyond this as some guidance depicts a \ncut-off low and associated sfc low developing offshore. \n\nThere won't be much in the way of appreciable CAA behind the \nfront, and above average temperatures may continue through next \nweekend. There remain no potential freezes in the forecast as \nthe more substantial cold air remains in the northern half of \nCanada for the foreseeable future.",
